  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\n\nWe are to find a step function h: [0,2] -> [0,1] with integral 1 that minimizes the maximum overlap integral C5 = max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx.\n\nOur approach involves:\n- Representing h by n_points samples on a uniform grid\n- Formulating it as a finite-dimensional optimization problem\n- Using CVXPY for convex optimization on projected and non-projected versions\n- Employing a bundle method for non-convex QCQP relaxation\n- Using exponential weighted coordinates moving average for stability\n\nThe method improves parent solution by:\na) Fixing point samples and optimizing smooth functionals\nb) Projecting onto convex sets while bounding the overlap\nc) Iteratively refining the solution using gradient information \n\nThe rationale is to find a step function that locally minimizes the maximum correlation, achieved by:\n- Transforming into a series of feasibility problems\n- Using efficient matrix-free methods than full matrix storage\n- Handling the huge constraint set through lower-dimensional bounds\n\nThis addresses the core difficulty: the combinatorial nature of step functions vs the continuous domain restrictions.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n\nThe problem is reduced to:\n\nmin t\ns.t. t \u2265 \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx, \u2200k\u2208\u211d\n     0\u2264h(x)\u22641 for all x\n     \u222bh(x)dx=1\n\nDiscretization uses dx=2/70, so:\n- Objective: t in \u211d^+\n- Constraints: Linear equality for integral and box constraints\n- Feasibility is equivalent to traveling salesman-like constraints\n\nFor optimization:\n- Apply exponential weights to gradient steps\n- Use l2 smoothness for potential barrier method\n- Form dual problem with 50-dimensional doubled support\n- Project onto L-infinity hypercube implicitly\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n\nPython/NumPy/SciPy/CVXPY Implementation:\n\n1. Input: initial_h_values (n=70)\n2. Define problem dimensions:\n   - n = 70 points\n   - dx = 2/70\n   - max_overlap = (current parent value: ~0.38)\n\n3. Battery of convex programs:\n   a) Projected gradient for step constraints\n   b) Duality from moment representation\n   c) Variable smoothing at boundary layers\n   d) Worst-case performance bound via Chebyshev\n\n4. Optimization Steps:\n   - Initialize with exponential weight vector w_i ~ Dirichlet(0.5, 0.05)\n   - For each iteration i=1 to num_it (\u224810):\n        * Compute gradient with respect to t\n        * Project onto feasible set (step function envelope)\n        * Apply nonsmooth penalty to integral constraint\n        * Use optimization on positive variables only\n        * Use l1 and l2 projections for stability\n\n5. Validation:\n   - Check 0<=h[i]<=1 for all i\n   - Validate sum(h)*dx=1\n   - Verify overlap constraints via matrix-free evaluation\n   - Use cross-validation against parent solution\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\n\nVariant 1: Progressive Smoothing\n- Coarse-to-fine resolution multiples: n=70, then n=700, then n=7000\n- Smoothness parameter: starting large, decaying exponentially with time\n\nVariant 2: Multi-solver Orchestration\n- Use SCS for equality constraints\n- Use IPOPT for quadratic relaxation\n- Use MOSEK for large-scale LP relaxations\n\nVariant 3: Underdetermined System Projection\n- Introduce redundant constraints for bound propagation\n- Project onto legality simplex via majorization\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\n\nTime Estimation:\n- Projection grid: 70 * 2 (forward-backward)\n- Each CVXPY problem: 8 seconds at base resolution\n- Total wall time estimate: 80 seconds for 10 iterations\n- Leaves >200 seconds for validation\n\nResource Notes:\n- CVXPY passing to MOSEK/Gurobi\n- Memory: 70x70 matrix storage for Hessian approximations\n- Hardware: Single core, no parallelization\n\nTermination Conditions:\n- Timelimit-based truncation\n- 10^-6 relative improvement slope\n- Gradient norm ~ 1e-10 threshold\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n\nFinal candidate checks:\n- Correct dimensionality (length 70)\n- Integral constraint: abs(sum(h)-35) < 0.1\n- Feasibility as step function: bins h[i] appropriately\n- Comparison to parent via overlap function\n- Handle boundary effects via clipping\n\nReturn format:\n(h_values, c5_bound, n_points)\nwhere h_values has properties:\n  - Length 70\n  - Both 0\u2264h[i]\u22641\n  - Sum(h) \u2248 35 (within 0.1)\n  - Evaluation: c5_bound = max_overlap",
